2025-05-01 00:56:42 - Thread-124 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Below are some exploratory Snowflake SQL statements – from very simple “peeking” queries to a complete (yet still exploratory) JOIN that would return the number of pull‑request creation events for 18 Jan 2023 in repositories that list JavaScript as one of their languages.  
All column / table names are wrapped in double quotes.  
Each query is limited to 20 rows (except the final COUNT) so you can safely inspect intermediate results.

```sql
-- 1) Description: Look at a few raw rows from the 2023‑events table.
SELECT *
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2023"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Description: Inspect the structure of the “repo” VARIANT and pull out the repository name.
SELECT 
       t."repo",
       t."repo":name::STRING AS "repo_name",
       t."created_at"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2023"  t 
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Description: Preview only the PullRequestEvent rows (all actions, all dates).
SELECT 
       t."repo":name::STRING  AS "repo_name",
       t."type",
       t."payload",
       t."created_at"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2023" t
WHERE  t."type" = 'PullRequestEvent'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Description: Check which “action” values exist inside payload for PullRequestEvent.
SELECT DISTINCT
       t."payload":action::STRING AS "pull_request_action"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2023" t
WHERE  t."type" = 'PullRequestEvent'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Description: Pull only PR‑creation (“opened”) events for a single calendar day: 2023‑01‑18.
-- (The numeric created_at format is inspected first; if it is epoch‑like, adjust comparison.)
SELECT 
       t."repo":name::STRING AS "repo_name",
       t."payload":action::STRING AS "action",
       t."created_at"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2023" t
WHERE  t."type"       = 'PullRequestEvent'
  AND  t."payload":action::STRING = 'opened'
  AND  TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ(t."created_at")) = '2023-01-18'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Description: Peek inside the LANGUAGES table; view how languages are stored.
SELECT 
       l."repo_name",
       l."language"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LANGUAGES" l
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Description: Identify repos that list JavaScript among their languages.
SELECT 
       l."repo_name"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LANGUAGES" l
WHERE  l."language" ILIKE '%JavaScript%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Description: Count how many repos in the languages table contain the substring “JavaScript”.
SELECT COUNT(*) AS "javascript_repo_count"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LANGUAGES" l
WHERE  l."language" ILIKE '%JavaScript%';
```

```sql
-- 9) Description: Join PR‑creation events (2023‑01‑18) with the languages table to see matches.
SELECT 
       pr."repo_name",
       COUNT(*) AS "pr_open_events_on_20230118"
FROM  (
       SELECT 
              t."repo":name::STRING AS "repo_name"
       FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2023" t
       WHERE  t."type"                 = 'PullRequestEvent'
         AND  t."payload":action::STRING = 'opened'
         AND  TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ(t."created_at")) = '2023-01-18'
      )  pr
JOIN   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LANGUAGES" l
       ON pr."repo_name" = l."repo_name"
WHERE  l."language" ILIKE '%JavaScript%'
GROUP  BY pr."repo_name"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Description: Final aggregation – total count of PR‑creation events on 18 Jan 2023
--                  across repos that list JavaScript in their language set.
SELECT 
       COUNT(*) AS "total_pr_creation_events_20230118_js_repos"
FROM  (
       SELECT 
              t."repo":name::STRING AS "repo_name"
       FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2023" t
       WHERE  t."type"                 = 'PullRequestEvent'
         AND  t."payload":action::STRING = 'opened'
         AND  TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ(t."created_at")) = '2023-01-18'
      ) pr
JOIN   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LANGUAGES" l
       ON pr."repo_name" = l."repo_name"
WHERE  l."language" ILIKE '%JavaScript%';
```

Explanation of potentially useful columns and their usage in this task:

• "repo":name → extracted as repo_name: lets us join with LANGUAGES.  
• "type": identifies event kind; we need 'PullRequestEvent'.  
• "payload":action: inside payload JSON tells whether the PR was 'opened'.  
• "created_at": timestamp / epoch number used to keep only 2023‑01‑18 rows.  
• "language": VARIANT in LANGUAGES; we search for the substring 'JavaScript'.  
• "repo_name": common key available (or derivable) in both event and language tables to perform the join.
